  :adr/title "ai-gftd-misogi — reversible macOS hygiene console (CleanMyMac-class cleaner as a cybersecurity product)"
  :adr/status "active"
  :adr/doc_type "adr"
  :adr/topic "cybersecurity"
  :adr/last_verified "2026-07-26"
  :adr/body "# Decision\n\nCreate `gftdcojp/ai-gftd-misogi` (禊 — ritual purification by washing) as the desktop hygiene console of the `ai-gftd-*` cybersecurity series, built on `kotoba-lang/shell` as its native host.\n\nThe product is CleanMyMac-class in surface (find junk, reclaim disk, uninstall leftovers, review startup items) but is designed as a **security product**, not a cleaner with a security badge. Three commitments make that difference real and testable:\n\n1. **Two scored axes, not one.** Every observed item carries both a `kegare` score (穢れ — junk: reclaimable bytes × staleness × safety) and a `tsumi` score (罪 — threat: persistence, signature, provenance, exposure). A 40 GB stale Xcode cache and a 12 KB unsigned LaunchAgent are both findings; they rank on different axes and are never collapsed into one \"junk\" number the way consumer cleaners do.\n2. **Deny-by-default admission.** Nothing enters a removal plan because a heuristic liked it. A candidate must (a) sit under an explicitly allowlisted root, (b) fail no protected-path predicate, and (c) be matched by a named rule that attaches its own evidence. Every plan line is traceable to a rule id.\n3. **No deletion. Ever.** `apply` moves items into a per-run quarantine vault with a full restore manifest; `restore` puts them back at their exact original paths with mode and mtime. Reclaiming the space is a separate, later, explicit `purge` gesture on a vault that has aged past a retention floor. This is simultaneously the safety-floor requirement (no destructive irreversible removal) and the correct engineering design for a tool that removes things a heuristic chose.\n\n# Why a new repo, and where it sits in the series\n\n`ai-gftd-mamori` is the *cross-platform, off-device* privacy map: which company receives which data from which app. `ai-gftd-misogi` is its *on-device, local-only* counterpart: what is actually on this machine, what persists across reboot, what holds TCC grants, and what can be reversibly removed. They share the vocabulary of recipients and permission classes and are intentionally not merged — mamori's data is owner-scoped cloud state, misogi's never leaves the device.\n\n`ai-gftd-kakure` (personal-data erasure) and `ai-gftd-hakken` (discovery) do not overlap: kakure erases records held by third parties, misogi quarantines files held locally.\n\n# Architecture\n\n```\nsrc/misogi/*.cljc          pure, IO-free core — the whole product's judgment\n  taxonomy   controlled vocabulary: item classes, persistence classes, TCC classes,\n             severity bands, evidence kinds\n  rules      the rule table as data (kegare rules + tsumi rules), each with id,\n             matcher, evidence extractor, base severity\n  score      kegare/tsumi scoring from rule hits + observed facts\n  policy     deny-by-default path admission (allow roots, protected predicates)\n  plan       facts + policy + rules -> ordered, reversible plan\n  ledger     append-only receipt construction\n  view       hiccup views (dual-render: kotoba-ui ->page HTML, and kotoba:dom ops)\n\nsrc/misogi/host/*.cljs     nbb — the only place IO exists\n  fs         bounded directory walk + stat (never follows symlinks out of root)\n  probe      launchd / login items / config profiles / codesign / spctl / xattr\n  vault      quarantine move + restore + purge, with manifest\n  cli        bin/misogi entry\n\nmisogi.app.kotoba.edn      kotoba-shell app manifest (native macOS window)\nmisogi.plugin.edn          kotoba-shell plugin: the provider commands misogi needs\n```\n\nThe pure core takes **observed facts** and returns **plans and scores**. It cannot read a disk, spawn a process, or delete anything — that is what makes the rule table auditable and the tests real.\n\n# Host surface: why a plugin, and why no listening socket\n\n`kotoba-lang/shell`'s provider catalog (`aiueos_provider_catalog.edn`) ships 9 provider families: ledger, fs-app-data, notification, clipboard, http-fetch, keychain, contacts, calendar, webauthn. `fs/*` is app-data scoped read/write/append text — there is no directory walk, no stat, no move, no process inventory. misogi therefore declares a kotoba-shell **plugin** (`misogi.plugin.edn`) contributing three read-mostly provider families:\n\n- `misogi/fs-survey`   — `fs/walk`, `fs/stat` (read-only, root-bounded)\n- `misogi/persistence` — `persistence/list`, `signature/verify` (read-only)\n- `misogi/vault`       — `vault/quarantine`, `vault/restore`, `vault/purge` (the only mutating family; move-only)\n\nThe native window dispatches UI actions through `KOTOBA_SHELL_ACTION_BIN` (stdin event -> stdout receipt), **not** through the `data-endpoint` HTTP path the mangaka exemplar uses. A security tool should not open a listening port on the machine it is auditing, and the action-bin path is already supported by `bin/kotoba-shell-app-runtime`.\n\n# Runtime placement\n\nThe repo rule orders runtimes kotoba wasm > clojurewasm > ClojureScript > nbb, with JVM last. misogi's first-class execution path is **nbb** for the CLI, survey, vault, and HTML report — the pure core is `.cljc` and runs there unchanged.\n\nThe native-window path is the exception and it is not misogi's choice: `kotoba-shell app run` evaluates an app entry with `clojure -M -e \"(require 'ns)(prn (ns/start))\"`, so the shell's app contract is JVM-hosted today. misogi ships a thin `src/misogi/app.clj` shim that requires the same `.cljc` core and returns `:kotoba.app/surface-ops`. No product logic lives in the shim. Moving the shell app entry off the JVM is upstream work in `kotoba-lang/shell`, tracked as a follow-up, not something misogi routes around by duplicating logic.\n\n# UI\n\nViews are pure `.cljc` hiccup and render two ways from one source: `kotoba-ui.core/->page` for the shareable HTML audit report (design-quality scored, per ADR-2607132300), and a hiccup -> `kotoba:dom` op lowering for the native window, which the macOS host renders with the `liquid-glass__panel` / `liquid-glass__button` classes it already understands. No raw hex, no ad-hoc font sizes: one theme map, HIG text styles (ADR-2607122200).\n\n**The two renderers are not equal fidelity, and the native one is the lesser.** Measured 2026-07-26 against this machine: the HTML report scores 100.00 on the deterministic design-quality HIG/WCAG audit (control: a deliberately bad page scores 30.96, so the auditor discriminates). The native window renders the same view correctly but through the shell's AppKit subset — `kotoba-ui.shell/grid` collapses to a vertical stack because the native renderer has no grid, and long hero titles clip rather than wrap. These are limits of `kotoba-lang/shell`'s macOS renderer, not of the view, and they are upstream work.\n\nThe shell's `--web-url` WKWebView path would give the native window full design-system fidelity, and was evaluated. It is rejected for now on two grounds: `WKWebView.load(URLRequest:)` does not load `file://` (it needs `loadFileURL:allowingReadAccessTo:`), so the report would have to be served over loopback HTTP — a listening socket on the machine being audited, which contradicts the action-bin decision above — and the shell's app runtime disables surface commits in web mode, so the action -> re-render loop would need a separate design. Revisit if the shell gains a `loadFileURL` path.\n\n# LLM containment\n\nAn advisor model may annotate findings in plain language. It may **not** author, reorder, or admit a plan line — plans come only from the deterministic engine, and the action bin rejects any plan whose lines lack a rule id and a policy admission receipt. This keeps the actor-pattern containment property without making the product depend on a model being available or correct.\n\n# Verification (2026-07-26, first implementation)\n\n66 tests / 168 assertions pass under nbb, including a real-filesystem quarantine -> restore round trip (temp tree, `MISOGI_HOME` redirected) that asserts mode and mtime survive.\n\nRun read-only against this machine: 209 findings in ~27 s — 26 tsumi, 183 kegare, 1.3 GB reclaimable. Full CLI pipeline exercised end to end against a misogi-owned fixture inside a genuinely allowlisted root: dry run changed nothing, `apply --yes` moved it, `vault` listed it held, `purge --yes` refused with `within-retention` at 0 days, `restore` returned it byte- and mtime-identical, and the append-only ledger recorded survey/plan/apply/refusal/restore.\n\nThe first real run produced four false-positive classes that unit tests could not have found, all now fixed with regression tests:\n\n1. **Every third-party root daemon scored critical.** `execFileSync` only exposes stderr on its error object, and `codesign -dv` writes its whole report to stderr and exits 0 — so every signature verdict came back `:unknown`. Fixed by using `spawnSync`. This was a real defect, not a tuning issue: the tool was reporting confidently on evidence it had never actually read.\n2. **The entire local toolchain was flagged as concealment.** `hidden-program?` matched any dot-directory, so every `.venv/bin/*`, `~/.local/bin/*` and `~/.cargo/bin/*` autostart entry read as hidden. Now a known-toolchain allowlist applies; `/tmp` and unrecognised dot-directories still match.\n3. **Empty launchd stubs scored as unsigned root daemons.** Several uninstallers leave a plist with no `Program` at all. A plist that runs nothing is no longer a persistence fact.\n4. **Coverage lost sources.** Two roots feeding one surface overwrote each other, so `:launch-agents` printed \"0 jobs in /Library/LaunchAgents\" while 37 findings from `~/Library/LaunchAgents` sat in the same report. `misogi.coverage` now degrades statuses instead of overwriting — a denial can never be masked by a later success.\n\nSeparately, the native smoke run caught a lowering bug that no unit test covered: kotoba-ui slots (`nav-bar :trailing`) hand back a *vector of sibling elements*, which the hiccup->ops walker read as `[tag & children]` and serialized an entire button into a tag name. Fragments are now distinguished from elements and spliced.\n\nThe recurring lesson is that the rules that matter here cannot be validated against synthetic facts. Every one of these was invisible until the tool ran against a machine that actually had 37 LaunchAgents on it.\n\n# Consequences\n\n- Disk is not reclaimed at `apply` time. Users who expect a cleaner to free space instantly will see space freed only after `purge`. This is deliberate and is surfaced in the UI as vault state, not hidden.\n- The vault costs disk equal to what was quarantined until purged; the default retention floor is 7 days.\n- Full Disk Access is required for the TCC and persistence surveys to be complete. Without it the survey reports reduced coverage explicitly rather than silently under-reporting.\n\n# Alternatives rejected\n\n- **Extend `ai-gftd-mamori`.** Rejected: mamori's core is owner-scoped cloud state over kotoba Datomic; misogi is local-only and must never ship an inventory of a user's disk anywhere.\n- **Unlink with an undo journal.** Rejected: an undo journal that references deleted bytes is not an undo. Move-to-vault is the only restore that actually restores.\n- **Single \"cleanliness\" score.** Rejected: it is precisely the collapse that lets consumer cleaners present a signed-but-stale cache and an unsigned persistence item as the same kind of problem.\n"}]
